## 5. Remaining ambiguity versus required operational inputs

**Remaining product ambiguity: none established for this unit contract.**

At the witnessed divisor, `0 < Q < k` and `C=1` cannot produce an admissible
positive native transfer. The exact specified behavior is rejection with Q/C
preserved. Plan 5.4 requires positive q<=Q, retains residue, rejects zero-credit
custody and pays all C when q==Q. It does not promise that q==Q is executable
at every divisor or that future source funding will make it executable.
PRD MIG-R36/MIG-AC41 preserve the unconsumed remainder; this mapping leaves it
recorded and subject to those same validity checks rather than erasing it.
It neither requires waiting nor promises eventual representability.

A fixed-native denominator would produce different credit on this physical
sequence. That comparison distinguishes normalization meanings; it does not
establish two conflicting mandatory requirements. The admitted contract
selects the gon basis explicitly and preserves total configured credit.
It introduces no ceiling-up custody, `min(n*k,Q)` clamp, excess-asset donation,
forfeiture, display-zero clearing or terminal dust write-off. Adding any such
rule would exceed this admission.

Actual numerical original allocations, qualifying wallet/origin records,
exact original gon/principal/reward allocations, known ETH/DTF components,
economic quote grouping, frozen funded snapshot and cross-representation
nonoverlap remain required operational datasets. Their absence is not filled
by the synthetic probe and is not itself a new product question. Unconfigured
or unsupported records must reject; this report approves no operational award.

